	abstract = {Suggests that phoneme segmentation is particularly difficult and more difficult than syllable segmentation. Direct evidence of a developmental ordering of syllable and phoneme segmentation abilities in the young child is presented. Using a task which required 135 preschool, kindergarten, and 1st-grade children to tap out the number of segments in spoken utterances, it was found that, though ability in both syllable and phoneme segmentation increased with grade level, analysis into phonemes was significantly harder and perfected later than analysis into syllables. The relative difficulties of the different units of segmentation are discussed in relation to reading acquisition. (25 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)},
